Thanks Chia-Ping for the summary! I'll take some time to refine the KIP as yo=
u suggested.

I do have one little question about this:

> 1) Client-Broker Protocol Compatibility: Ensure seamless interoperability b=
etween 5.x clients and brokers, as well as with clients and brokers in the [4=
.x, 6.x] range.

When we're talking about two major versions, should it be [4.x, 5.x] or [4.x,=
 6.x]? The latter seems to refer to three major versions, doesn't it?"


On 2025/01/19 10:15:11 Chia-Ping Tsai wrote:
> Dear all,
>=20
> > only support upgrades between 2 major version, and also=20
> limit client-broker compatibility to two major version.
>=20
> I wholeheartedly support this concept due to its inherent simplicity. Howev=
er, a primary concern arises regarding third-party client libraries implement=
ed in other languages. These libraries serve as the primary reference for KIP=
-896, as I understand it.
>=20
> Furthermore, I propose expanding the definition of 'compatibility' to encom=
pass the following aspects:
>=20
> 1) Client-Broker Protocol Compatibility: Ensure seamless interoperability b=
etween 5.x clients and brokers, as well as with clients and brokers in the [4=
.x, 6.x] range.
>=20
> 2) Upgrade Path: Guarantee a smooth upgrade path from 4.x clients and broke=
rs to the 5.x version.
>=20
> The following points require further consideration:
>=20
> 1) Removal of Old Protocols: Should we remove protocols prior to 4.0 in the=
 5.0 release? This action could streamline the codebase, but we should carefu=
lly weigh the potential benefits against the need to accommodate users runnin=
g Kafka with older releases (even if there is no official guarantee)
>=20
> 2) Hybrid Broker-Broker Scenarios: How will the system behave in hybrid env=
ironments where 4.x, 5.x, and 6.x brokers coexist? Specifically, what happens=
 when a 6.x broker is introduced into a 5.x cluster that includes 4.x brokers=
? If we intend to remove older protocols in major releases, such hybrid confi=
gurations might need to be disallowed.
>=20
> 3) Downgrade Path: Supporting downgrades from 5.x clients and brokers to 4.=
x versions may pose challenges, particularly when considering the introductio=
n of new records.
>=20
> To Kuan-Po
>=20
> I recommend reorganizing KIP-1124 to include a dedicated section for discus=
sing the proposed new compatibility policy. Given that we anticipate a series=
 of discussions on this topic within this thread, incorporating a summary of =
the current discussion within the KIP itself would be highly beneficial.
>=20
> Best,
> Chia-Ping
>=20
>=20
> On 2025/01/16 11:31:15 Bruno Cadonna wrote:
> > Thanks for the KIP!
> >=20
> > I have to admit that all these different aspects of compatibility are=20
> > really confusing. When we talk about compatibility, we should try to be=20
> > precise about what kind of compatibility we talk about so that for users =

> > and developers it is clear.
> >=20
> > Basically, we have Java/Scala API compatibility and protocol API (i.e.=20
> > client and inter-broker RPC) compatibility.
> >=20
> > Java/Scala APIs:
> >=20
> > "Any incompatible changes should be ultimately settled in the KIP design =

> > process, where the usual strategy is to retain the old APIs, mark them=20
> > as deprecated and potentially remove them in some next major release."[1]
> >=20
> > At the moment, as far as I understand, this KIP proposes to document=20
> > what KIP-896 proposes and what was accordingly implemented. I do not=20
> > believe, we need a KIP for that. The intent of this KIP should be to=20
> > agree on a general policy when to remove old client protocol versions=20
> > (as Matthias pointed out). I found the following in [1]:
> >=20
> > "Our policy is that the Kafka protocols and data formats should support=20
> > backwards compatibility for as many releases to enable no-downtime=20
> > upgrades (unless there is a very compelling counter-argument). This=20
> > means the server MUST be able to support requests from both old and new=20
> > clients simultaneously. This compatibility needs to be retained for at=20
> > least one major release (e.g. 2.0 must accept requests from 1.0=20
> > clients). A typical upgrade sequence for binary format changes would be=20
> > (1) upgrade server to handle the new message format, (2) upgrade clients =

> > to use the new message format."
> >=20
> > It says "at least one major release (e.g. 2.0 must accept requests from=20
> > 1.0 clients)". I understand that Matthias wants to change "at least" to=20
> > "exactly". I guess, there might always be the option to write a KIP to=20
> > retain compatibility with older versions of certain protocols.
> >=20
> > So the question seems to be whether
> >=20
> > 1. we want to make the removal of old protocol versions automatically=20
> > (or at least not guarantee the existence) and write KIPs for retaining=20
> > certain old versions of protocols
> >=20
> > OR
> >=20
> > 2. we want to write KIPs to remove old protocol version (e.g. KIP-896).
> >=20
> > IMO, from a code maintenance perspective the former is preferable, from=20
> > a user perspective maybe the latter. However, I am not sure it is really =

> > preferable for users of clients to be able to postpone upgrades for too=20
> > long.
> >=20
> > So, I am in favor of 1.
> >=20
> > Best,
> > Bruno
> >=20
> >=20
> > [1] https://kafka.apache.org/coding-guide
> >=20
> > On 16.01.25 00:31, Matthias J. Sax wrote:
> > > Thanks for starting this. It's overdue that we define a public contract=
=20
> > > how far back we want do guarantee for direct upgrades.
> > >=20
> > > In general, a lot of information is in the docs (https://=20
> > > kafka.apache.org/39/documentation/streams/upgrade-guide) but the=20
> > > information is hard to extract. And it's not documented "how far back" =

> > > on could actually go.
> > >=20
> > > About Sophie's point: while important, it seems to be orthogonal to the=
=20
> > > question on hand? Also, the requirement of a two-round rolling bounce=20
> > > upgrade is not unique to the rebalance protocol.
> > >=20
> > > While it's great to define this for already release versions, to me it'=
s=20
> > > actually more important to look into the future, to set expectations=20
> > > right. (For the past, it's not really possible to just "drop" guarantee=
s=20
> > > suddenly...)
> > >=20
> > > Historically, we did support backward compatibility across multiple=20
> > > major versions (as long as no APIs are use which might have been=20
> > > removed). However, it think we should cut this down to two major=20
> > > versions going forward. Ie, we won't guarantee that a 3.x app can be=20
> > > upgraded to 5.x, but only 4.x can be upgrade to 5.x.
> > >=20
> > > It's really painful to maintain a longer compatibility window, and=20
> > > testing it is also very expensive.
> > >=20
> > > Furthermore, and this is an even broader question, we guarantee client-=
=20
> > > broker backward/forward compatibility across multiple major version,=20
> > > too. I think we should also limit client-broker compatibility to two=20
> > > major versions only. For 4.0 brokers, we already drop support for 2.0=20
> > > and older clients (and reverse, 4.0 client require 2.1+ brokers), what =

> > > is still a larger window.
> > >=20
> > > But I think for 5.0 brokers, we should only guarantee support for 4.0+ =

> > > clients and drop 2.x and 3.x... -- And in revers, if upgrading client t=
o=20
> > > 5.x, they would require broker 4.0, and we don't guarantee that 5.x=20
> > > clients work against 3.x/2.x brokers any longer.
> > >=20
> > > Given that we did 3 years of release with 2.x and 3.x each, I would=20
> > > expect that 5.x will also be like 3 years in the future, and 3 years is=
=20
> > > already a very long compatibility guarantee, and with two major version=
,=20
> > > 6 year old clients can still connect to the latest broker and vice-vers=
a.
> > >=20
> > > Of course, if it would really happen that we bump the major version=20
> > > quicker (like we did for 1.0/1.1 to 2.0), we can always add an exceptio=
n=20
> > > to the rule and document it accordingly. But IMHO, the general rule=20
> > > should be, to only support upgrades between 2 major version, and also=20
> > > limit client-broker compatibility to two major version.
> > >=20
> > > Having a public contract like this, should also avoid that we need to d=
o=20
> > > KIP to drop older RPC from the implementation when a new=C2=A0 major re=
lease=20
> > > comes along streamlining the development process.
> > >=20
> > >=20
> > > Thoughts?
> > >=20
> > >=20
> > > -Matthias
> > >=20
> > >=20
> > > On 1/15/25 3:00 PM, Sophie Blee-Goldman wrote:
