ue with the configuration of the `bootstrap.servers` property. =C2=A0
> In previous versions, it was possible to configure the `bootstrap.servers=
` with space-separated URLs, like this: =C2=A0
> {code:java}
> bootstrap.servers: "140.2.15.25:9092 140.2.15.26:9092 140.2.15.27:9092"=
=20
> {code}
> =C2=A0
> However, with version {*}3.8.0{*}, this configuration results in the foll=
owing exception: =C2=A0
> =C2=A0
> {code:java}
> org.apache.kafka.common.config.ConfigException: Invalid url in bootstrap.=
servers: 140.2.15.25:9092 140.2.15.26:9092 140.2.15.27:9092 {code}
> =C2=A0
> =C2=A0
> The configuration only works correctly if the URLs are separated by comma=
s, as shown below: =C2=A0
> =C2=A0
> {code:java}
> bootstrap.servers: "140.2.15.25:9092,140.2.15.26:9092,140.2.15.27:9092" {=
code}
> =C2=A0
> =C2=A0
> This behavior appears to be a change from previous versions where space-s=
eparated URLs were accepted. =C2=A0
> *Reproducible Example:*
> To demonstrate the issue, I created a minimal project that reproduces the=
 problem. You can find it here: =C2=A0
> [GitHub Repository|https://github.com/BeatrizRodrigues/kafka-config-test]
> *Question:* =C2=A0
> Is this the expected behavior for version *3.8.0* ? Was support for space=
-separated `bootstrap.servers` values intentionally deprecated, or is this =
a bug introduced in the latest release? =C2=A0
> Looking forward to clarification on whether this is an intentional change=
 or a bug. Thank you!



